WEBVTT

00:00:32.874 --> 00:00:34.341
Good news, everyone.

00:00:34.419 --> 00:00:38.046
The university is bringing me up
on disciplinary charges.

00:00:38.630 --> 00:00:40.591
Wait. That's not good news at all.

00:00:40.674 --> 00:00:44.177
Whatever you did, Professor,
I'm sure there's a reasonable explanation.

00:00:44.261 --> 00:00:46.179
Yes, but they won't listen.

00:00:46.263 --> 00:00:49.433
Everyone's always in favor
of saving Hitler's brain,

00:00:49.516 --> 00:00:52.227
but when you put it in the body
of a great white shark,

00:00:52.311 --> 00:00:54.646
ooh, suddenly, you've gone too far.

00:01:05.490 --> 00:01:07.492
Professor Farnsworth.

00:01:07.576 --> 00:01:09.995
Do you know why
we've called you here today?

00:01:10.078 --> 00:01:12.289
Listen to me, you pompous frauds.

00:01:12.372 --> 00:01:15.500
If I'm going down,
I'm taking you all with me.

00:01:15.584 --> 00:01:17.836
Dean Vernon, I know the truth.

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:22.257
It was you driving your hover car
that night, not your horse.

00:01:22.341 --> 00:01:28.263
Dean Epsilon, I know all about
your Department of Pool Boy Studies.

00:01:28.347 --> 00:01:29.806
And Dr. Wornstrum...

00:01:30.724 --> 00:01:32.309
Wornstrum...

00:01:32.392 --> 00:01:35.646
Actually, Professor, we merely
called you here to say...

00:01:35.729 --> 00:01:36.813
Surprise!

00:01:36.897 --> 00:01:39.441
- Happy birthday!
- Surprise! Happy birthday!

00:01:39.524 --> 00:01:40.651
Surprise!

00:01:40.859 --> 00:01:42.694
And you, Coach Smalley,

00:01:42.778 --> 00:01:45.822
or should I say, "Coach Hairpiece?"

00:01:45.906 --> 00:01:48.700
No, Professor. It's a surprise party

00:01:48.784 --> 00:01:50.869
for your 150th birthday.

00:01:51.453 --> 00:01:52.913
What? Oh!

00:01:55.999 --> 00:01:57.668
Good evening. I'm...

00:01:58.210 --> 00:01:59.294
Whoa. Sorry.

00:01:59.378 --> 00:02:02.005
I'm Bender,
and I'll be emceeing this roast.

00:02:02.089 --> 00:02:05.509
You know, they say you can judge a man
by the company he keeps.

00:02:05.592 --> 00:02:10.597
So here's the professor's oldest friend,
a grotesque, stinking lobster.

00:02:13.517 --> 00:02:15.477
Good evening, ladies and germs.

00:02:16.728 --> 00:02:18.021
That wasn't a joke.

00:02:18.105 --> 00:02:20.399
I was talking to Dean Streptococcus.

00:02:21.191 --> 00:02:24.653
Now, I'm not saying
Professor Farnsworth is old,

00:02:24.736 --> 00:02:28.240
but if you consider his age,
he's likely to die soon.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:32.452
Hey, Ringo, that was the joke.

00:02:33.787 --> 00:02:37.165
It's Showtime at the Apollo
all over again.

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:42.170
Where would a professor be
without students who love and respect him?

00:02:42.588 --> 00:02:44.464
Right there!

00:02:44.881 --> 00:02:49.386
But, seriously, of all the former crew
members of the professor's delivery ship

00:02:49.469 --> 00:02:52.681
our next speaker is by far the most alive.

00:02:52.764 --> 00:02:53.765
Captain Musky?

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:08.363
I wouldn't want to follow that guy.

00:03:11.283 --> 00:03:14.244
And now a man who needs no introduction.

00:03:20.167 --> 00:03:21.585
- Fry! Get up there!
- Hmm?

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:25.631
Thank you.

00:03:25.714 --> 00:03:27.716
You know, when I was first asked
to make a film

00:03:27.799 --> 00:03:30.010
about my nephew, Professor Farnsworth...

00:03:30.093 --> 00:03:31.762
I thought, "Why should I?"

00:03:31.845 --> 00:03:33.305
Then later, Leela made the film,

00:03:33.388 --> 00:03:34.848
but if I had made the film,

00:03:34.932 --> 00:03:37.851
you can bet there would have been
more topless women on motorcycles.

00:03:37.935 --> 00:03:39.102
Roll film.

00:03:46.526 --> 00:03:50.447
Hubert J Farnsworth
was born April 9, 2851,

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:54.326
in New New York's nerdiest slum,
Hell's Laboratory.

00:03:54.409 --> 00:03:57.579
A precocious child,
young Hubert learned to read

00:03:57.663 --> 00:04:00.374
while he was still in diapers,
at age eight,

00:04:00.457 --> 00:04:04.419
and before long, he blossomed
into a greasy teenager.

00:04:04.503 --> 00:04:05.545
Dork alert!

00:04:05.629 --> 00:04:07.839
After 14 years of graduate school,

00:04:07.923 --> 00:04:11.134
Farnsworth settled into the
glamorous life of a scientist.

00:04:11.218 --> 00:04:14.805
Fast cars. trendy nightspots,
beautiful women,

00:04:14.888 --> 00:04:19.560
the professor designed them all working
out of his tiny one-room apartment.

00:04:19.643 --> 00:04:23.105
For 50 years, he worked
at Mom's Friendly Robot Company

00:04:23.188 --> 00:04:27.526
where he created the first robot
capable of qualifying for a boat loan.

00:04:27.609 --> 00:04:31.780
And now, even as he nears
his 150th birthday,

00:04:31.863 --> 00:04:35.033
the professor retains
the fiery passion of youth.

00:04:35.117 --> 00:04:37.286
Listen to me, you pompous frauds!

00:04:37.369 --> 00:04:40.539
If I'm going down,
I'm taking you all with me.

00:04:47.629 --> 00:04:49.631
How about a few words, Professor?

00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:53.468
I said "words."

00:04:56.388 --> 00:04:57.389
Uh...

00:04:57.472 --> 00:05:01.143
What a pleasure it is
to see my lifetime of accomplishment

00:05:01.226 --> 00:05:03.729
summed up in a three-minute film.

00:05:04.354 --> 00:05:06.857
My best years are behind me,

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:11.862
so much left undone, so little time.

00:05:17.242 --> 00:05:19.328
Funny, funny stuff.

00:05:21.830 --> 00:05:23.832
My life is over.

00:05:23.916 --> 00:05:25.208
No, it isn't.

00:05:25.292 --> 00:05:27.002
You have another 10 years left.

00:05:27.085 --> 00:05:30.130
Leela, he could live another 100 years.

00:05:30.672 --> 00:05:32.382
No, he couldn't.

00:05:32.466 --> 00:05:34.051
When you turn 160,

00:05:34.134 --> 00:05:37.846
robots from the Sunset Squad
take you to a mysterious planet,

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:39.431
and you never return.

00:05:39.514 --> 00:05:42.726
Wow. A whole planet of old people.
Where is it?

00:05:42.809 --> 00:05:44.603
Nobody knows.

00:05:44.686 --> 00:05:49.566
So many loves half-loved,
so many inventions half-invented.

00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:53.654
That damn time machine alone
set me back 15 years.

00:05:53.779 --> 00:05:55.989
If only it had worked,

00:05:56.073 --> 00:05:58.367
you could go back
and not waste your time on it.

00:05:58.450 --> 00:06:01.036
There's no one to carry on
after I'm gone,

00:06:01.119 --> 00:06:05.958
no one to take over my work
and my research and my fabulous fortune.

00:06:06.083 --> 00:06:08.126
By God, that's it!

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:10.087
I've got to name a successor.

00:06:10.170 --> 00:06:11.630
A successor?

00:06:11.713 --> 00:06:13.632
A successor to the professor?

00:06:13.715 --> 00:06:15.050
There's no time to lose.

00:06:15.133 --> 00:06:19.680
I'm off to my lab to build
a successor-naming machine.

00:06:21.682 --> 00:06:24.142
Man, the professor's
been in his lab for days.

00:06:24.226 --> 00:06:25.394
I hope he didn't die.

00:06:25.477 --> 00:06:27.729
unless he left a note
naming me his successor.

00:06:27.813 --> 00:06:28.897
Then I hope he did die.

00:06:28.981 --> 00:06:32.109
You the successor?
Over my empty shell.

00:06:32.192 --> 00:06:36.697
The professor will pick me.
Only I have his lobster-like tenacity.

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:38.365
Up yours, Zoidberg.

00:06:38.448 --> 00:06:41.577
Up wherever your species
traditionally crams things.

00:06:41.660 --> 00:06:45.664
The only sensible way to pick a successor
is with a limbo contest.

00:06:45.747 --> 00:06:47.291
- What?
- Kingston rules.

00:06:47.374 --> 00:06:49.418
Two men go down, one come up.

00:06:52.713 --> 00:06:55.924
Look, the professor trusts me
with a giant spaceship.

00:06:56.008 --> 00:06:58.176
He wouldn't trust
the rest of you with his dentures.

00:06:58.260 --> 00:06:59.469
Yes, he would.

00:06:59.553 --> 00:07:01.305
Sorry, everyone, but need I remind you?

00:07:01.388 --> 00:07:03.515
Blood is thicker than water.

00:07:03.599 --> 00:07:06.643
"Blood, thicker, water."

00:07:09.062 --> 00:07:11.815
Everyone, I have
a very dramatic announcement.

00:07:11.899 --> 00:07:14.735
So anyone with a weak heart
should leave now.

00:07:15.319 --> 00:07:16.320
Goodbye.

00:07:17.321 --> 00:07:18.697
Uh, Professor?

00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:21.199
Oh, yes, the announcement.

00:07:21.283 --> 00:07:24.119
As you all know,
I am not long for this world.

00:07:24.202 --> 00:07:25.704
- Yes, we do.
- True that.

00:07:25.787 --> 00:07:27.497
- Yeah, I know.
- One foot in the grave.

00:07:27.581 --> 00:07:29.625
So I've picked my successor.

00:07:29.708 --> 00:07:32.044
It's someone in whom I have great faith,

00:07:32.127 --> 00:07:35.547
even though his mind is undeveloped
and he's accomplished nothing.

00:07:37.257 --> 00:07:40.219
- My closest living relative...
- Oh, yeah.

00:07:40.302 --> 00:07:43.513
...my clone, Cubert Farnsworth.

00:07:56.151 --> 00:07:57.277
He's horrible!

00:07:57.945 --> 00:08:00.739
Crud. Can I at least be
in charge of your dentures?

00:08:00.822 --> 00:08:02.032
You wish.

00:08:06.119 --> 00:08:07.579
Where did Cubert come from?

00:08:07.663 --> 00:08:10.415
Twelve years ago,
I began the cloning process

00:08:10.499 --> 00:08:12.459
by removing some skin cells

00:08:12.542 --> 00:08:15.254
from one of the
shapelier growths on my back.

00:08:15.337 --> 00:08:18.882
Wait. If he's your clone,
why doesn't his nose look like yours?

00:08:18.966 --> 00:08:23.262
I left him in his first tube too long,
and he got squished up against the side.

00:08:23.345 --> 00:08:25.013
Is he dumb or just ugly?

00:08:25.097 --> 00:08:26.431
Let's find out.

00:08:35.190 --> 00:08:38.026
What, you've never seen
a genius's wiener before?

00:08:38.110 --> 00:08:40.487
- No. Never.
- Well, once in the park.

00:08:48.203 --> 00:08:49.204
Mmm...

00:08:49.329 --> 00:08:54.126
As long as I'm going to be in charge here,
let me examine my so-called crew.

00:08:54.209 --> 00:08:56.211
If it can so be called.

00:08:56.295 --> 00:09:01.049
First of all, Dr Zoidberg,
do you even have a medical degree?

00:09:01.133 --> 00:09:04.011
I lost it in a volcano.

00:09:04.094 --> 00:09:07.681
And why do we need a bending robot
around here anyway?

00:09:07.764 --> 00:09:10.017
What possible use do we have for you?

00:09:10.183 --> 00:09:12.269
Uh... Me no speaka the English.

00:09:12.352 --> 00:09:15.606
And why does our space pilot
have only one eye?

00:09:15.689 --> 00:09:17.316
There's someone I'd like you to meet.

00:09:17.399 --> 00:09:20.652
His name is depth perception.

00:09:20.819 --> 00:09:22.362
Why, you little...

00:09:23.530 --> 00:09:26.283
Wow! That hurt. The air.

00:09:26.366 --> 00:09:28.952
Him pending para un bending.

00:09:29.036 --> 00:09:30.495
Oh, Cubert, come in here.

00:09:30.579 --> 00:09:32.664
I have something amazing to show you.

00:09:32.748 --> 00:09:35.083
What is it? A competent employee?

00:09:35.167 --> 00:09:37.669
I doubt that very much.

00:09:38.712 --> 00:09:39.963
Little twerp.

00:09:40.047 --> 00:09:41.506
Ay, Chihuahua!

00:09:43.133 --> 00:09:47.471
Cubert, as my successor,
I'm trusting you to carry on my work.

00:09:47.554 --> 00:09:50.224
These are just some of
the half-finished inventions

00:09:50.307 --> 00:09:51.934
you'll spend your life finishing.

00:09:52.017 --> 00:09:55.562
I didn't realize you were the inventor
of the junk heap.

00:09:55.646 --> 00:09:58.273
That's my prize-winning smell-a-scope.

00:09:58.357 --> 00:10:03.320
If a dog craps anywhere in the universe,
you can bet I won't be out of the loop.

00:10:03.403 --> 00:10:06.949
And this is my universal translator.

00:10:07.032 --> 00:10:12.537
Unfortunately, so far, it only translates
into an incomprehensible dead language.

00:10:12.621 --> 00:10:13.956
- Hello.
- Bonjour.

00:10:14.039 --> 00:10:15.123
Crazy gibberish.

00:10:15.207 --> 00:10:17.668
Don't you have any worthwhile inventions?

00:10:17.751 --> 00:10:20.462
Why, certainly. Step over here.

00:10:20.545 --> 00:10:23.799
These are the dark-matter
engines I invented.

00:10:23.882 --> 00:10:29.012
They allow my starship
to travel between galaxies in mere hours.

00:10:29.096 --> 00:10:32.599
That's impossible. You can't go
faster than the speed of light.

00:10:32.683 --> 00:10:33.892
Of course not.

00:10:33.976 --> 00:10:38.105
That's why scientists increased
the speed of light in 2208.

00:10:38.188 --> 00:10:39.439
Also impossible.

00:10:39.523 --> 00:10:43.277
And what makes my engines truly remarkable
is the afterburner,

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:46.113
which delivers 200% fuel efficiency.

00:10:46.196 --> 00:10:48.240
That's especially impossible.

00:10:48.323 --> 00:10:50.534
Not at all. It's very simple.

00:10:50.617 --> 00:10:51.952
Then explain it.

00:10:52.035 --> 00:10:53.662
Now, that's impossible.

00:10:53.745 --> 00:10:57.457
It came to me in a dream,
and I forgot it in another dream.

00:10:57.541 --> 00:11:01.670
Your explanations are pure
weapons-grade bologna-ium.

00:11:01.753 --> 00:11:03.422
It's all impossible.

00:11:03.505 --> 00:11:06.466
Nothing is impossible.
Not if you can imagine it.

00:11:06.550 --> 00:11:09.303
That's what being a scientist
is all about.

00:11:09.386 --> 00:11:12.931
No, that's what being
a magical elf is all about.

00:11:17.936 --> 00:11:19.021
Oh, my God! Oh, my God!

00:11:19.146 --> 00:11:20.939
It's Elzar, the TV chef!

00:11:21.023 --> 00:11:22.441
Oh, kill me now, people.

00:11:22.524 --> 00:11:23.650
How we doin' here?

00:11:23.734 --> 00:11:27.112
Oh, Elzar! Everything's so good.

00:11:27.195 --> 00:11:29.114
What are you, an ass-kissing machine?

00:11:29.197 --> 00:11:30.991
Yes, sir. Good one, sir.

00:11:31.158 --> 00:11:33.452
Oh, it's a gem of an evening.

00:11:33.585 --> 00:11:37.839
I feel so wonderful having someone
to take over my life's work,

00:11:37.923 --> 00:11:40.384
and it's all thanks to Cubert.

00:11:40.801 --> 00:11:44.262
Look, Professor, I may be identical
to you in every possible way,

00:11:44.346 --> 00:11:46.890
but that doesn't mean
I'm anything like you.

00:11:46.974 --> 00:11:50.102
- Yeah, well... What?
- I don't want to be an inventor.

00:11:50.185 --> 00:11:51.687
I want to be something useful,

00:11:51.770 --> 00:11:56.608
like a teacher's aide or a prison guard
or a science fiction cartoon writer.

00:11:56.692 --> 00:12:02.114
But what about my hopes and my dreams
and my wonderful inventions?

00:12:02.197 --> 00:12:06.159
In your entire life,
your only half-decent invention was me,

00:12:06.243 --> 00:12:08.203
and I didn't turn out
like you wanted either.

00:12:13.292 --> 00:12:14.835
You folks still doing all right?

00:12:14.918 --> 00:12:16.336
Oh, yes, Elzar.

00:12:16.420 --> 00:12:18.755
Good. 'cause it turns out
I forgot to cook that chicken.

00:12:24.303 --> 00:12:25.721
Bad news, everyone.

00:12:25.804 --> 00:12:29.099
By the time you watch this tape,
I'll be gone,

00:12:29.182 --> 00:12:34.062
leaving behind nothing but a history
of failure and my original hip bones.

00:12:34.146 --> 00:12:36.857
You see,
I've been lying about my age.

00:12:36.940 --> 00:12:39.067
I'm not actually 150.

00:12:39.151 --> 00:12:41.069
I'm 160.

00:12:41.153 --> 00:12:44.573
Oh, Vanity, thy name
is Professor Farnsworth.

00:12:44.656 --> 00:12:46.867
and now that I have nothing to live for,

00:12:46.950 --> 00:12:51.121
I've alerted the Sunset Squad
robots to take me away.

00:13:18.899 --> 00:13:21.318
Goodbye, cruel world.

00:13:23.570 --> 00:13:25.530
Goodbye, cruel lamp.

00:13:27.407 --> 00:13:29.743
Goodbye, cruel velvet drapes

00:13:29.826 --> 00:13:33.455
lined with what would appear to be
some sort of cruel muslin

00:13:33.538 --> 00:13:34.873
and the cute little...

00:13:34.998 --> 00:13:37.084
...pom-pom curtain pull-cords.

00:13:37.167 --> 00:13:39.169
Cruel though they may be, I...

00:13:43.048 --> 00:13:45.050
...and now
that I havenothing to live for,

00:13:45.133 --> 00:13:49.346
I've alerted the Sunset Squad
robots to take me away.

00:13:50.681 --> 00:13:51.682
Oh, no!

00:13:51.765 --> 00:13:53.392
I know you're all very upset,

00:13:53.475 --> 00:13:54.559
especially Bender.

00:13:54.643 --> 00:13:56.770
Well, life goes on.

00:13:56.853 --> 00:13:58.647
Except for you.

00:13:59.606 --> 00:14:02.359
I'm sure Bender
has just made a cutting remark,

00:14:02.442 --> 00:14:06.571
but he doesn't know I taped over
his soap operas to record this message.

00:14:06.655 --> 00:14:08.115
You bastard!

00:14:08.198 --> 00:14:09.950
We've got to get him back.

00:14:10.033 --> 00:14:11.076
Impossible.

00:14:11.159 --> 00:14:13.203
No one knows where
they take those old geezers.

00:14:13.287 --> 00:14:14.538
Nothing is impossible.

00:14:14.621 --> 00:14:17.874
You'd know that if you really
took after the professor, like I do.

00:14:17.958 --> 00:14:19.501
You're his uncle, dummy.

00:14:19.584 --> 00:14:20.794
He takes after you.

00:14:20.877 --> 00:14:22.004
Yeah, what?

00:14:22.087 --> 00:14:25.966
Wait a second.
That means I also take after you. Ahhh!

00:14:26.049 --> 00:14:28.468
Quiet. I think I know
how to find the professor.

00:14:28.552 --> 00:14:30.053
Lay it on us, big boots.

00:14:31.763 --> 00:14:34.308
If the smell-a-scope
can pick up the professor's odor,

00:14:34.391 --> 00:14:36.059
we may have a chance to save him.

00:14:36.226 --> 00:14:38.186
I think not.

00:14:38.270 --> 00:14:40.439
As you probably already don't know,

00:14:40.522 --> 00:14:44.651
odors are made up of particles that
can't travel through the vacuum of space.

00:14:44.776 --> 00:14:45.902
Ahhh!

00:14:45.986 --> 00:14:47.487
I'm zeroing in on him.

00:14:48.071 --> 00:14:49.072
Bengay,

00:14:49.156 --> 00:14:50.407
mothballs,

00:14:51.450 --> 00:14:52.701
letters to the editor!

00:14:52.784 --> 00:14:54.202
It's the professor!

00:14:54.286 --> 00:14:56.371
To the flying machine!

00:15:00.292 --> 00:15:01.293
To the left!

00:15:01.376 --> 00:15:02.377
No, to the up!

00:15:02.836 --> 00:15:04.129
U-turn! U-turn!

00:15:04.212 --> 00:15:06.048
We'll never find this place.

00:15:06.131 --> 00:15:08.675
Robots are very good at keeping secrets.

00:15:08.759 --> 00:15:10.552
No, we're not, you little bed wetter!

00:15:10.636 --> 00:15:11.637
Oops, I'm sorry.

00:15:11.720 --> 00:15:13.847
There it is! The near death star.

00:15:28.111 --> 00:15:31.490
Okay, we'll tell them the professor
escaped, and we're bringing him back.

00:15:31.573 --> 00:15:34.701
Fry, you'll have to dress up
like a 160-year-old man.

00:15:34.785 --> 00:15:35.786
I'm on it.

00:15:37.788 --> 00:15:38.830
My God!

00:15:38.914 --> 00:15:40.749
The illusion is so perfect.

00:15:40.832 --> 00:15:43.085
I almost forgot I was
looking at an idiot.

00:15:43.168 --> 00:15:45.212
Now, they may ask for a DNA sample.

00:15:45.295 --> 00:15:47.506
I'd like to see them find it.

00:15:47.589 --> 00:15:49.383
This plan is impossible.

00:15:49.466 --> 00:15:52.094
We don't even have a sample
of the professor's DNA.

00:15:52.177 --> 00:15:54.179
I think I know
where to get some.

00:16:02.896 --> 00:16:05.190
Why do I have to be the hump?

00:16:05.274 --> 00:16:06.942
'Cause you're too ugly to be a wart.

00:16:33.760 --> 00:16:35.637
Your Medicare dollars at work.

00:16:36.722 --> 00:16:39.349
Halt! Identify this guest.

00:16:39.433 --> 00:16:42.769
Uh, this is Professor Hubert Farnsworth.
He escaped.

00:16:42.853 --> 00:16:45.105
Escaped? No one escapes.

00:16:45.188 --> 00:16:47.941
This guest does not look 160.

00:16:48.025 --> 00:16:49.818
What, I'm old! Listen...

00:16:49.901 --> 00:16:52.487
Hey, you kids, get off the lawn!

00:16:52.613 --> 00:16:55.407
Hmm, it is true old people
are often concerned

00:16:55.490 --> 00:16:56.867
that there are children on their lawns.

00:16:56.950 --> 00:16:58.201
There's no denying that,

00:16:58.285 --> 00:17:01.663
but we'll still need to verify
his identity with a DNA sample.

00:17:01.747 --> 00:17:03.999
Got a hot, steaming batch right here.

00:17:05.292 --> 00:17:06.710
We only needed one cell.

00:17:06.793 --> 00:17:09.921
- Eh, keep the change, buddy.
- Stupid robot.

00:17:10.005 --> 00:17:11.381
Did your hump just say something?

00:17:12.049 --> 00:17:14.009
I've got talking hump syndrome.

00:17:14.092 --> 00:17:15.677
Ah, THS.

00:17:18.847 --> 00:17:20.057
Identity confirmed.

00:17:20.140 --> 00:17:22.893
Return this shambling shuffleboarder
to his room.

00:17:23.602 --> 00:17:25.437
7152, Maple Drive.

00:17:25.520 --> 00:17:27.898
- Sounds nice.
- Prepare to be surprised.

00:17:40.953 --> 00:17:44.289
So this is where they stick old people.
It's horrific.

00:17:44.373 --> 00:17:46.083
At least it keeps them from driving.

00:18:00.263 --> 00:18:02.140
Brace yourself for the worst.

00:18:05.560 --> 00:18:07.312
And yet he looks so natural.

00:18:07.396 --> 00:18:08.438
What's happening?

00:18:08.522 --> 00:18:10.399
He's hooked up to a life-support system.

00:18:10.482 --> 00:18:15.070
We have to disconnect him very, very
carefully, or the shock could kill him.

00:18:15.445 --> 00:18:16.446
Freeze!

00:18:28.667 --> 00:18:29.668
Seize them!

00:18:29.751 --> 00:18:30.752
- Seize them!
- Seize them!

00:18:30.836 --> 00:18:32.754
Get them! I mean, seize them!

00:18:39.303 --> 00:18:40.637
Oh, I'm so bad at this.

00:18:51.273 --> 00:18:52.274
Uh-oh.

00:19:06.413 --> 00:19:08.874
We're probably gonna make it,
but we might not!

00:19:08.957 --> 00:19:10.876
It's impossible! We'll never fit!

00:19:10.959 --> 00:19:12.753
Will too.

00:19:14.004 --> 00:19:15.005
Told you.

00:19:22.512 --> 00:19:25.307
Come on, Leela.
Step your big boot down on the gas pedal.

00:19:40.989 --> 00:19:42.574
They've blown out one of our engines.

00:19:42.658 --> 00:19:45.327
Fix it, fix it,
fix it, fix it, fix it, fix it!

00:19:45.410 --> 00:19:46.620
Fix it, fix it, fix it!

00:19:46.703 --> 00:19:48.789
Only the professor knows how to fix it.

00:19:48.872 --> 00:19:50.082
We have to wake him up.

00:19:54.795 --> 00:19:55.796
Try shocking him.

00:19:55.879 --> 00:19:58.006
Your social security check is late!

00:19:58.090 --> 00:20:00.217
Stuff costs more than it used to!

00:20:00.300 --> 00:20:02.761
Young people use curse words!

00:20:02.844 --> 00:20:05.138
Damn it.
We'll have to fix the engine ourselves.

00:20:05.222 --> 00:20:06.765
We can't, you bastard.

00:20:06.848 --> 00:20:08.517
No one knows how it works.

00:20:08.600 --> 00:20:09.768
It's impossible!

00:20:09.851 --> 00:20:11.853
Nothing is impossible.

00:20:11.937 --> 00:20:14.022
I understand how the engines work now.

00:20:14.106 --> 00:20:15.315
It came to me in a dream.

00:20:15.399 --> 00:20:17.985
The engines don't move the ship at all.

00:20:18.068 --> 00:20:19.736
The ship stays where it is,

00:20:19.820 --> 00:20:22.239
and the engines
move the universe around it.

00:20:22.322 --> 00:20:23.699
That's a complete load.

00:20:23.782 --> 00:20:27.077
Nothing's a complete load.
Not if you can imagine it.

00:20:27.160 --> 00:20:30.163
That's what being a scientist
is all about, right, Professor?

00:20:32.457 --> 00:20:33.458
Let's ride.

00:20:48.765 --> 00:20:50.475
Good news, everyone.

00:20:50.559 --> 00:20:52.269
He's made a complete recovery.

00:20:52.352 --> 00:20:53.687
All right! Way to go!

00:20:53.812 --> 00:20:57.149
I'm as spry as a 140-year-old.

00:20:57.274 --> 00:20:59.526
See? I only broke one ankle.

00:20:59.610 --> 00:21:01.945
So what were they doing to you
in that awful drawer?

00:21:02.112 --> 00:21:05.907
Oh, they had me hooked up
to a bizarre virtual world

00:21:05.991 --> 00:21:07.534
that seemed absolutely real.

00:21:07.618 --> 00:21:08.619
What was it like?

00:21:08.702 --> 00:21:11.413
It was as though I were living
in a facility in Florida

00:21:11.496 --> 00:21:13.415
with hundreds of other old people.

00:21:13.498 --> 00:21:18.170
All day long, we'd play bingo, eat oatmeal
and wait for our children to call.

00:21:18.337 --> 00:21:20.505
It's a hundred times more horrible

00:21:20.589 --> 00:21:22.174
than anything I could imagine.

00:21:22.257 --> 00:21:23.508
Oh, my, yes.

00:21:23.592 --> 00:21:27.763
Thank you all for saving me,
especially you, my little clone.

00:21:27.846 --> 00:21:30.307
No matter what
you decide to do with your life,

00:21:30.390 --> 00:21:32.351
I'm still proud of you.

00:21:33.018 --> 00:21:34.561
I've already decided.

00:21:35.312 --> 00:21:39.441
Dad, when I grow up,
I want to be just like you.

00:21:39.524 --> 00:21:41.234
Don't worry, son.

00:21:41.318 --> 00:21:42.319
You will.

00:21:42.986 --> 00:21:48.325
Incidentally, you might want to read up
on a condition known as wandering bladder.

00:21:48.408 --> 00:21:49.409
Why?

00:21:49.493 --> 00:21:51.078
No reason.

00:21:51.161 --> 00:21:52.746
No reason at all.